=====Usage notes=====
Though certain uses of "sentence-initial so" had been common for a long time, the perceived excessive use of the word at the start of sentences, such as at the start of answers to questions, became controversial in the 2010s, being described as "annoying".<ref>{{RQ:en:National Public Radio|date=September 3, 2015|author=w:Geoff Nunberg|url=https://www.npr.org/2015/09/03/432732859/so-whats-the-big-deal-with-starting-a-sentence-with-so|title=So, What's The Big Deal With Starting A Sentence With 'So'?|ascite=1}}</ref><ref>{{cite-web|url=https://www.spectator.co.uk/article/it-s-so-annoying|work=w:The Spectator|title=It’s so annoying|author=Mark Manson|date=05 November 2011|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20251207200500/https://www.spectator.co.uk/article/it-s-so-annoying/|archivedate=7 December 2025}}</ref>

